  "database": "schemabench_0206",
  "nl_text": "The schemabench_0206 database is designed to manage user interactions and relationships within a social networking platform. The users table stores information about each registered user, including their unique user ID, name, password, email, phone number, address, and registration date. The accounts table links user IDs to their respective account types and descriptions, while the posts table stores the content and posting history of each user, along with the post type and date. The comments table contains user comments on posts, with each comment linked to the post and the user who made it, as well as the date it was made. The likes table tracks which users have liked which posts, and the friendship table stores friendships between users, including the date they became friends. The messages table stores private messages between users, with each message linked to the sender and receiver, along with the message text and date, and a flag indicating whether it has been read. The notifications table tracks notifications sent to users, including the notification text and date, as well as a flag indicating whether it has been read. The groups table stores information about groups, including their name, creation date, and the user who created them, while the admins table links group administrators to the groups they manage and the users who are part of those groups. The group_members table stores information about which users are part of each group, including the date they joined.",
  "gold_question": "1) Background / Context:\n\nThe project involves the development of a social media platform. This platform is designed to facilitate user interaction and content sharing, leveraging various social media functionalities such as posting, commenting, liking, and messaging. The system architecture is based on a relational database schema, which interlinks multiple data tables to capture user activities and social connections.\n\n2) Objectives:\n\n- To create a robust social media database (`SocialMediaDatabase`) that supports essential social networking functionalities.\n- To ensure scalable and secure user interactions, including content creation, communication, and social engagement.\n- To facilitate efficient data management and retrieval through relational links among tables.\n\n3) Stakeholders & Users:\n\n- End Users: Individuals who will register and actively participate on the platform by sharing content, interacting with other users, and managing personal accounts.\n- Administrators: Users with elevated permissions to oversee platform activities, manage user-generated content, and moderate group interactions.\n- Developers: Technical personnel responsible for designing, implementing, and maintaining the database and platform functionalities.\n\n4) Functional Descriptions:\n\n- User Management: Users can register, log in, and manage personal profiles. Registration information is stored in the `users` table.\n- Account Management: Users can have different types of accounts, managed via the `accounts` table and linked to the main `users` table.\n- Content Creation & Interaction:\n  - Users can create posts (`posts` table) and others can comment on them (`comments` table).\n  - Users can like posts (`likes` table) to express appreciation.\n- Social Connections: \n  - Users can form friendships (`friendship` table).\n  - Direct communication is facilitated through private messaging (`messages` table).\n- Group Interactions:\n  - Users can create and join groups (`groups_` and `group_members` tables).\n  - Groups can have administrators assigned to manage group activities (`admins` table).\n- Notifications: Users receive notifications (`notifications` table) about various activities involving their account.\n  \n5) Non-Functional Descriptions:\n\n- Scalability: The system should support a growing number of users and content without performance degradation.\n- Security: User data, particularly passwords, must be securely stored and managed.\n- Reliability: The platform should ensure data integrity and availability, preventing loss or corruption of user data.\n- Usability: The system interfaces should be intuitive, promoting ease of use for users with varying technical expertise.",
  "input_json": "/data/SchemaBench/Experiment/schema2text_llama31/0206.json",
  "ddl": "-- Schema for database: schemabench_0206\nCREATE TABLE `users` (\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `password` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `email` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `phone_number` varchar(20) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `address` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  `registration_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`user_id`)\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `accounts` (\n  `account_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `account_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `description` varchar(255) DEFAULT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`account_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `accounts_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `posts` (\n  `post_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `content` text NOT NULL,\n  `post_type` varchar(50) NOT NULL,\n  `post_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`post_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `posts_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `comments` (\n  `comment_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `post_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `comment_text` text NOT NULL,\n  `comment_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`comment_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `comments_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`post_id`) REFERENCES `posts` (`post_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `comments_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `likes` (\n  `like_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `post_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`like_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `likes_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`post_id`) REFERENCES `posts` (`post_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `likes_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `friendship` (\n  `friendship_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id_1` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id_2` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `friendship_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`friendship_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `friendship_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id_1`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `friendship_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id_2`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `messages` (\n  `message_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `sender_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `receiver_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `message_text` text NOT NULL,\n  `message_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `is_read` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`message_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `messages_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`sender_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `messages_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`receiver_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `notifications` (\n  `notification_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `notification_text` text NOT NULL,\n  `notification_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `is_read` tinyint(1) NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`notification_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `notifications_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `groups` (\n  `group_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `group_name` varchar(255) NOT NULL,\n  `creation_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  `creator_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`group_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `groups_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`creator_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `admins` (\n  `admin_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `group_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`admin_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `admins_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`group_id`) REFERENCES `groups` (`group_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `admins_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;\n\nCREATE TABLE `group_members` (\n  `membership_id` int(11) NOT NULL AUTO_INCREMENT,\n  `group_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `user_id` int(11) NOT NULL,\n  `join_date` datetime NOT NULL,\n  PRIMARY KEY (`membership_id`),\n  CONSTRAINT `group_members_ibfk_1` FOREIGN KEY (`group_id`) REFERENCES `groups` (`group_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE,\n  CONSTRAINT `group_members_ibfk_2` FOREIGN KEY (`user_id`) REFERENCES `users` (`user_id`) ON DELETE CASCADE ON UPDATE CASCADE\n) ENGINE=InnoDB DEFAULT CHARSET=utf8mb3;",
